1
00:00:00,998 --> 00:00:03,248
(no audio)

2
00:00:10,501 --> 00:00:13,918
(choral classical music)

3
00:00:22,832 --> 00:00:24,450
- The president could sense the arrival

4
00:00:24,450 --> 00:00:26,850
of his chief of staff
from the slight breeze

5
00:00:26,850 --> 00:00:28,473
the door makes when opened.

6
00:00:29,310 --> 00:00:32,010
And the president wished he would knock.

7
00:00:32,010 --> 00:00:34,117
And the chief of staff thought,

8
00:00:34,117 --> 00:00:37,500
"I'm not the kind of guy
who knocks and waits."

9
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:40,377
And the president thinks,
"I wish he would wait."

10
00:00:41,530 --> 00:00:44,011
But the chief of staff ignores this-

11
00:00:44,011 --> 00:00:48,840
- Cheese, American and feta, tuna,

12
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:53,620
vodka for my office,
olives, dried berries.

13
00:00:57,570 --> 00:00:58,893
Let's stop you there.

14
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:01,800
Any comments?

15
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:02,793
- I loved it.

16
00:01:03,720 --> 00:01:05,490
- That's one, I loved it.

17
00:01:05,490 --> 00:01:08,073
How about any constructive criticisms?

18
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:12,930
Going once, twice, moving on.

19
00:01:12,930 --> 00:01:15,180
- I mean, you could,
you could say something.

20
00:01:16,680 --> 00:01:18,870
- I've given you my thoughts before.

21
00:01:18,870 --> 00:01:20,970
Just have a glance at your last story,

22
00:01:20,970 --> 00:01:22,260
or the one before that.

23
00:01:22,260 --> 00:01:23,480
Okay, Jen, would you like to read-

24
00:01:23,480 --> 00:01:24,990
- It would spike some discussion

25
00:01:24,990 --> 00:01:27,573
if you offered something out loud.

26
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:29,460
- I don't think so.

27
00:01:29,460 --> 00:01:30,293
- It could.

28
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:37,530
Could you please just
for once say something?

29
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:40,020
- Well, workshops like
this are based on the idea

30
00:01:40,020 --> 00:01:42,840
of peer review.
- Guided review.

31
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:44,520
- Trying to guide.

32
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:45,480
Jen.

33
00:01:45,480 --> 00:01:48,690
- Professor, I don't
mean to gang up on you,

34
00:01:48,690 --> 00:01:52,770
but you should note, besides
calling our names to read

35
00:01:52,770 --> 00:01:55,653
you've barely said anything
in an hour and a half.

36
00:01:59,130 --> 00:02:02,160
- Well, I couldn't help but
notice a wandering point of view

37
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:04,770
within basically structural sentences.

38
00:02:04,770 --> 00:02:07,590
- No, my point of view doesn't wander.

39
00:02:07,590 --> 00:02:10,710
- Well, it switches
mid-sentence from the president

40
00:02:10,710 --> 00:02:12,870
to the chief of staff,
then back then back and...

41
00:02:12,870 --> 00:02:14,820
- Yeah, on purpose.

42
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:17,280
- Well, you risk distancing the reader.

43
00:02:17,280 --> 00:02:20,343
- I happen to think that
risk is worth the reward.

44
00:02:21,270 --> 00:02:22,710
- What's the reward?

45
00:02:22,710 --> 00:02:27,710
- Okay, the reward is
I'm exposing the flow of

46
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:32,760
different collected
consciousness. You know?

47
00:02:32,760 --> 00:02:34,800
I'm calling it ultra omniscience.

48
00:02:34,800 --> 00:02:36,510
- That's a cool word.

49
00:02:36,510 --> 00:02:38,610
Did anyone else care that the president

50
00:02:38,610 --> 00:02:40,590
and chief of staff seemed to be responding

51
00:02:40,590 --> 00:02:44,040
to each other's thoughts without speaking

52
00:02:44,040 --> 00:02:47,430
in a way that isn't possible?

53
00:02:47,430 --> 00:02:49,440
- I mean, I'm just gonna
say it again, I loved it,

54
00:02:49,440 --> 00:02:52,020
and you made my shortlist
for the Pulitzer.

55
00:02:52,020 --> 00:02:53,880
- You don't get to make a shortlist

56
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:55,080
for someone else's award.

57
00:02:55,080 --> 00:02:57,600
- Yeah, look, responding
to each other's thoughts,

58
00:02:57,600 --> 00:02:58,433
it's not a mistake.

59
00:02:58,433 --> 00:02:59,400
- It certainly was.

60
00:02:59,400 --> 00:03:01,350
Let's put aside the
multiple points of view

61
00:03:01,350 --> 00:03:03,780
and the wandering tense
and the conversations

62
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:05,550
that seem to happen via telepathy,

63
00:03:05,550 --> 00:03:07,860
and even put aside the chief
of staff's necrophilia.

64
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:08,970
- Okay, well, then there's no story.

65
00:03:08,970 --> 00:03:11,370
- Do you know what I mean when
I say distance the reader?

66
00:03:11,370 --> 00:03:14,160
- Of course I do, but
that's my choice to make,

67
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:18,150
as it is every writer's,
as it was Chaucer's choice.

68
00:03:18,150 --> 00:03:21,090
You know, who's writing I
actually find really distancing.

69
00:03:21,090 --> 00:03:22,830
- Yeah, well, less so
because he was writing

70
00:03:22,830 --> 00:03:25,140
in middle English and it's a tricky thing

71
00:03:25,140 --> 00:03:26,760
comparing yourself to Chaucer.

72
00:03:26,760 --> 00:03:29,310
- Yeah, but we don't know
that I'm not the next Chaucer.

73
00:03:29,310 --> 00:03:31,410
- We do know.
- All due respect,

74
00:03:31,410 --> 00:03:32,310
you would not know.

75
00:03:32,310 --> 00:03:34,263
- All due respect, a cat would know.

76
00:03:35,520 --> 00:03:36,990
- Your only novel isn't even available

77
00:03:36,990 --> 00:03:39,150
at your own campus bookstore.

78
00:03:39,150 --> 00:03:42,870
- You, you're here, you're here.

79
00:03:42,870 --> 00:03:45,480
The main piece of evidence
is that you are here.

80
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:46,710
The fact that you're here means that

81
00:03:46,710 --> 00:03:48,420
you didn't try very hard in high school,

82
00:03:48,420 --> 00:03:51,990
or for whatever reason, you
show very little promise.

83
00:03:51,990 --> 00:03:53,460
Did that sound harsh?

84
00:03:53,460 --> 00:03:55,680
Tell you what, I'll smile
through the rest of this.

85
00:03:55,680 --> 00:03:58,350
You are here and even if your presence

86
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:00,930
at this middling college
in this sad, forgotten town

87
00:04:00,930 --> 00:04:02,310
was some bizarre anomaly

88
00:04:02,310 --> 00:04:04,530
and you do have the promise of genius,

89
00:04:04,530 --> 00:04:06,990
which I'll bet a kidney that you don't,

90
00:04:06,990 --> 00:04:09,750
it will never surface.

91
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:13,830
I am not a good enough
writer or writing teacher

92
00:04:13,830 --> 00:04:15,450
to bring it outta you.

93
00:04:15,450 --> 00:04:17,370
And how do I know that? How?

94
00:04:17,370 --> 00:04:22,370
'Cause I too am here at Railton College,

95
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:26,373
mediocrity's capital.

96
00:04:30,660 --> 00:04:33,900
Well, you wanted me to talk more.

97
00:04:33,900 --> 00:04:36,567
(upbeat music)

